findings.body:
The write used to happen inside `_run()`, so a missing row or a DB error failed the node and the workflow. Now it runs in `on_event`. `EventManager._notify_layers` wraps every layer call in `try/except Exception` and only logs it. The node therefore reports `SUCCEEDED` even though `ConversationVariableNotFoundError` or a DB error means the conversation variable was never saved. The user sees a successful run, and later turns read the stale value.

::diff{file="api/core/app/layers/conversation_variable_persist_layer.py" lines="52-53"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The behavioural change is silent. Nothing in the PR description or tests mentions it. If swallowing is acceptable, document it. If not, the node or the engine needs a way to surface layer write failures.

::file{file="api/core/workflow/graph_engine/event_management/event_manager.py"} lines 177-186 are where the exception is caught.

Before this PR, v1 raised `VariableOperatorNodeError("conversation_id not found")`, and v2 raised `ConversationIDNotFoundError` for any non-debugger invocation. The PR deletes those checks along with the nodes' writes. The layer now returns early with no log and no error:

::code{file="api/core/app/layers/conversation_variable_persist_layer.py" lines="34-36"}

If an assigner writes a conversation variable without a conversation id, the value changes in the pool and in the run output but is never saved. That is the data-integrity failure the old checks existed to prevent. Keep the error or at least log a warning.

The layer is attached only in `AdvancedChatAppRunner`. `WorkflowEntry.single_step_run` (`api/core/workflow/workflow_entry.py`) builds its own `GraphEngine` and attaches only `DebugLoggingLayer`, `ExecutionLimitsLayer`, and `ObservabilityLayer`. The draft-run path in `services/workflow_service.py` (~L653) creates a real conversation id through `get_or_create_conversation` and passes it into the variable pool. Before this PR, a single-step run of a Variable Assigner wrote to the `ConversationVariable` row. Now it does not. The draft value appears to update, but the stored conversation variable stays unchanged. Debugging and the next real chat then disagree.

::diff{file="api/core/app/apps/advanced_chat/app_runner.py" lines="204-205"}

I confirmed that `single_step_run` attaches no conversation layer. I did not run the debug flow end to end, so treat this as a strong finding that needs a test.

`ConversationVariableUpdaterImpl.update` replaces `with Session(db.engine) as session:` with a bare `session = Session(db.engine)`. Nothing closes it, and nothing rolls it back when the row is not found. Each assigner event therefore leaves a checked-out connection until garbage collection. Under load, pool exhaustion is possible. The not-found path also leaves an uncommitted session open.

::diff{file="api/services/conversation_variable_updater.py" lines="18-23"}

The previous context-managed form was correct. Restore it.
